<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yodo-dono</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1569">1569</a>–<a href="/years/1615">1615</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Yodogimi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yodo-dono, born <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-felix-de-souza">Chacha</a>, was the eldest daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azai-nagamasa">Azai Nagamasa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oichi">Oichi</a> and became a consort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>. She bore his heir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> Hideyori, and after Hideyoshi's death led the Toyotomi faction at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a>. She died with her son when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> in 1615.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yodo-dono.jpg" alt="Yodo-dono" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 奈良県立美術館収蔵『傳 淀殿畫像』 / Nara Museum of Art · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yodo-dono_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Chacha was the eldest of three daughters of the daimyō <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azai-nagamasa">Azai Nagamasa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oichi">Oichi</a>, the younger sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>. When Nobunaga's army besieged Odani Castle in 1573, she, her mother and her sisters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohatsu">Ohatsu</a> and Oeyo were sent out safely, and her father and her only brother, Manpukumaru, died in its fall. In 1583 Oichi died with her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shibata-katsuie">Shibata Katsuie</a> when he set fire to Kitanosho Castle under siege by Hideyoshi, having first placed her daughters in Hideyoshi's care. Ohatsu later married Kyōgoku Takatsugu, and Oeyo became the wife of the shōgun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-hidetada">Tokugawa Hidetada</a> and mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-iemitsu">Tokugawa Iemitsu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When she became pregnant in 1588, Hideyoshi, who had no sons, was greatly pleased, and in 1589 he gave her Yodo Castle, remodelled by his brother Toyotomi Hidenaga. Her first son, Tsurumatsu, died in 1591, and her second, Hideyori, born in 1593, became Hideyoshi's designated successor. After Hideyoshi's death in 1598 she took tonsure as the nun Daikōin, founded the temple Yogen-in in memory of her parents and became the effective head of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a> as Hideyori's guardian. In 1600 she and three commissioners wrote urging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> to hasten to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a>'s rising, and she gave the Western Army no warrant in Hideyori's name; Ieyasu used her letter to brand Ishida's cause a rebellion.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> became shōgun in 1603 she resisted him, refusing in 1605 to let Hideyori pay homage. The inscriptions on the bell of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuka-dera">Hōkō-ji</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> read as a curse on Ieyasu, and the gathering of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronin">rōnin</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> deepened the quarrel, and she banished Katagiri Katsumoto, who had proposed sending her to Edo as a hostage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the siege of 1614 she hired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronin">rōnin</a> with gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> when no daimyō would help, and took part in the defence before agreeing to a peace. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> attacked again in 1615 she and Hideyori killed themselves as the castle burned. No eyewitness account survives and her body was never found, which gave rise to stories of escape to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a> or Utsunomiya. Writers seeking to exalt the Tokugawa often portrayed her as a wicked and wanton woman, and James Clavell's Shōgun drew its Lady Ochiba from her.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yodo-dono” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Brazilian merchants such as Francisco Félix de Souza, known as the <b>Chacha</b>, became powerful intermediaries in the early nineteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dahomey">Kingdom of Dahomey · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>His daughters, <b>Chacha</b>, Hatsu and Go, later shaped Toyotomi and Tokugawa politics.</blockquote><a href="/state/azai-clan-domain">Azai clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Odani Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.46,136.28&amp;year=1598" aria-label="Odani Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7996 882 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yodo-dono"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8446 1152h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8446 1152h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.46,136.28&amp;year=1598">What was here in 1598? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
